🧠 Analysis

Subnetting was correctly implemented:

Each device has a valid IP address within its subnet
Each device can reach its default gateway

However:

👉 Since LAN-A and LAN-B are in different subnets, communication between them requires Layer 3 routing (router).

🔍 Troubleshooting Steps
Step 1: Verify Local Connectivity

PC-A → Gateway

ping 192.168.0.1

✅ Success

PC-B → Gateway

ping 192.168.0.65

✅ Success

✔️ Conclusion: Subnetting and local configuration are correct.

Step 2: Test Inter-Subnet Communication
ping 192.168.0.126

❌ Failed

👉 Indicates a Layer 3 (routing) issue.

Step 3: Check Router Interfaces
show ip interface brief

Result:

G0/0 → up/up
G0/1 → administratively down ❌
🛠 Solution

Enable the router interface connected to LAN-B:

configure terminal
interface g0/1
no shutdown
end
🔁 Verification
ping 192.168.0.126

✅ Success

🎯 Root Cause

The router interface for LAN-B was shutdown, preventing routing between subnets.

🧠 Key Insight

Subnetting created multiple isolated networks.

✔️ Local communication worked (within each subnet)
❌ Inter-subnet communication failed due to missing routing

👉 Subnetting requires proper routing to enable communication between networks.

📌 Key Takeaway

"Subnetting creates separate networks, but routing enables communication between them."
